  "hints": [
    "Group the subarrays by where they end. What is the combined contribution of every subarray that ends at index `i`?",
    "Inside that group, a subarray either stops before reaching any element smaller than `nums[i]` (so the minimum is `nums[i]`), or it stretches past the nearest strictly smaller element to the left, in which case its minimum was already the minimum of something ending there. Find that index.",
    "A stack holding indices with strictly increasing values gives you the nearest smaller index in amortized constant time. Pop values equal to the current one as well, so ties are always charged to one side."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes a start index and walks right with a running minimum, so every one of the O(n\u00b2) subarrays pays a single comparison. The optimal groups subarrays by their right endpoint. Let `dp[i]` be the sum of the minima of all subarrays ending at `i`, and let `j` be the nearest index to the left of `i` whose value is strictly smaller than `nums[i]`. Subarrays that start at `j + 1` or later cannot reach `nums[j]`, so `nums[i]` is their minimum, which is `i - j` subarrays contributing `nums[i] * (i - j)`. Subarrays that start at or before `j` contain the smaller `nums[j]`, so their minimum is exactly the minimum they already had at `j`, contributing `dp[j]`. Hence `dp[i] = dp[j] + nums[i] * (i - j)`, or `nums[i] * (i + 1)` when no smaller element exists to the left. A monotonic increasing stack of indices yields `j` in amortized O(1): pop every index whose value is `>= nums[i]`, so the surviving top is guaranteed to be strictly smaller.",
    "why_optimal": "Each index is pushed once and popped at most once, so all the stack work across the scan is linear and the answer accumulates in the same pass. Every subarray influences the result, and the only way to finish in less than quadratic time is to count them in groups rather than enumerate them, which the dp-over-right-endpoint split does exactly. n <= 200 makes the quadratic version legal to submit, but the linear one is what gets asked for at scale.",
    "pitfalls": "Popping with `>` instead of `>=` leaves equal values on the stack, so subarrays are attributed to two different minimums and the total inflates; equal values must be popped on both sides so each subarray is counted once. When the stack is empty the base case is `nums[i] * (i + 1)`, not `nums[i]`. The running minimum in the brute force must be initialized to `nums[i]`, not to 0, or arrays of negative numbers come out wrong. Accumulate `dp[i]` into the answer as you go (the accumulator starts at 0, which also covers a length-zero input). Watch the arithmetic width on large inputs: the exact sum can exceed 2^31 for long arrays, though the given bounds keep it far below that."
  },
